// Practice quiz: Seeing the generated SQL
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/spring-data-jpa/seeing-the-generated-sql

::o1 logger::Which logger shows the SQL statements Hibernate issues? {
=org.hibernate.SQL at debug#Correct. It logs each statement.
~org.springframework.web at debug#Not quite. That logs web request handling.
~java.sql.Driver at info#Not quite. That is not the statement logger.
~org.hibernate.stat at debug#Not quite. That is for statistics.
}

::o1 bind values::A statement in the log shows question marks. How do you see the actual values? {
=Set org.hibernate.orm.jdbc.bind to trace#Correct. That logger reports bound parameters.
~Set show-sql to true again#Not quite. That prints the same placeholders.
~Change the SQL to concatenate strings#Not quite. That creates injection risk.
~Restart in debug mode only#Not quite. The specific logger must be on.
}

::o2 matching::A service loads a row and calls one setter inside a transaction, with no explicit save. Which write appears, and when? {
=An update, at flush near commit#Correct. Dirty checking finds the change.
~An insert, at the setter#Not quite. The row already exists, and setters do no SQL.
~An update, at the setter#Not quite. A setter runs no SQL.
~No write ever#Not quite. Managed entity changes are written at flush.
}

::o3 extra query::What does an unexplained select in a log usually mean? {
=Some code triggered it, often by reading a lazy association#Correct. Queries do not appear from nowhere.
~The log is random#Not quite. Hibernate logs what it runs.
~The database added it itself#Not quite. The database runs what it is given.
~It can always be ignored#Not quite. It may be a real cost.
}

::o3 production caution::Why keep bind-value logging out of production? {
=Bound values may contain personal or secret data#Correct. Logs spread widely.
~It makes SQL invalid#Not quite. It only logs.
~It is unavailable in production#Not quite. It is available, which is the risk.
~It disables transactions#Not quite. It does not.
}

